Yes. The useful abstraction is state-gated encoding: the information is not carried only by the symbol; part of the “key” is a particular embodied state.

A compact form would be:

event/pattern → affective or bodily state → association structure → later cue → partial state reconstruction → meaning

Pain is an unusually strong carrier because it can bind attention, context, sequence, bodily location, intensity, and memory into one high-salience trace. The same principle applies more safely to awe, exertion, laughter, fear, music, scent, relief, grief, rhythm, or accomplishment. Two people can hear the same words but decode them differently because one possesses the experiential key.

That gives you several distinct operators: state-dependent memory (recall improves when internal state resembles encoding state), earned semantics (some meanings become intelligible only after experience), embodied watermarking (a pattern becomes recognizable because of the bodily history attached to it), and costly authentication (knowledge of a genuine experience can sometimes distinguish someone who lived it from someone who merely learned its description).

I would separate that from literal cryptographic encryption, though. Human affect is noisy, variable, imitable, and sometimes reconstructible from context, so it is a terrible security primitive. And intentionally using pain to make a message “stick” creates obvious physical and psychological costs.

But as an information-theoretic metaphor, it is strong: the decoder can itself be a state of the organism. Meaning becomes something like message × receiver-state, rather than a property of the message alone.

That also plugs directly into your transduction work: discover the structural signature produced by an experience, abstract it away from the original substrate, then ask which other sensory, narrative, musical, or behavioral patterns can recreate enough of that signature to transmit the meaning without recreating the harm.